            <ArticleTitle>Malignant orbital schwannoma with massive intracranial recurrence.</ArticleTitle>
            <Pagination>
                <MedlinePgn>1291-4; discussion 1294</MedlinePgn>
            </Pagination>
            <ELocationID EIdType="doi" ValidYN="Y">10.1007/s00701-008-0159-3</ELocationID>
            <Abstract>
                <AbstractText Label="BACKGROUND" NlmCategory="BACKGROUND">A 62 year old male presented with progressive diplopia, left orbital pain and impairment of visual acuity.</AbstractText>
                <AbstractText Label="METHOD AND FINDINGS" NlmCategory="RESULTS">Neuroradiological investigation disclosed an orbital tumour. The lesion was totally excised. Histopathology examination revealed a malignant peripheral nerve sheath tumour (MPNST). The tumour recurred with intracranial extension. The patient died 13 months after the initial diagnosis.</AbstractText>
                <AbstractText Label="CONCLUSIONS" NlmCategory="CONCLUSIONS">To our knowledge, this is the first reported example of a massive intracranial recurrence of an orbital MPNST. The epidemiological features, clinical course and treatment of these lesions are discussed.</AbstractText>
            </Abstract>
